# udisksctl — real-world examples

Userspace disk management via udisks2 — mount removable drives without
sudo or fstab entries. On Tumbleweed it ships with the `udisks2` package.

## Inspect devices

```sh
# Get info about a block device
udisksctl info -b /dev/sdb1

# Overview of all drives and partitions
udisksctl status

# Monitor events live (plug in a USB stick and watch)
udisksctl monitor
```

## Mount and unmount

```sh
# Mount a device — no sudo needed; lands under /run/media/david/<label>
udisksctl mount --block-device /dev/sdb1

# Unmount it
udisksctl unmount --block-device /dev/sdb1

# Power off a USB drive safely before unplugging
udisksctl unmount -b /dev/sdb1
udisksctl power-off -b /dev/sdb
```

## Loop devices and images

```sh
# Mount an ISO without sudo (e.g. checking an openSUSE install image)
udisksctl loop-setup -f openSUSE-Tumbleweed-DVD-x86_64.iso
udisksctl mount -b /dev/loop0

# Tear it down
udisksctl unmount -b /dev/loop0
udisksctl loop-delete -b /dev/loop0
```

## Encrypted volumes

```sh
# Unlock a LUKS partition (prompts for passphrase), then mount the mapper device
udisksctl unlock -b /dev/sdc1
udisksctl mount -b /dev/dm-0

udisksctl lock -b /dev/sdc1
```

## Get help

```sh
udisksctl help
```

## Subcommand reference

| Subcommand | Purpose |
|------------|---------|
| `status` | list drives |
| `info -b DEV` | detailed info on a device |
| `mount / unmount -b DEV` | mount/unmount without sudo |
| `loop-setup -f FILE` | attach an image as a loop device |
| `unlock / lock -b DEV` | LUKS open/close |
| `power-off -b DEV` | safely power down a drive |
| `monitor` | watch udisks events live |
